Without a context, `info.context` is `None`, so the `isinstance` check prevents filtering. With `context={'stopwords': [...]}`, the serializer removes those words [pydantic/concepts/serialization.md].

Implement `__get_pydantic_core_schema__` on the class. Return a `core_schema` that uses a plain string schema as its input, wrapped in an after-validator that builds your class. Don't call `handler(source)` for custom types, since Pydantic can't generate a schema for them and would raise `PydanticSchemaGenerationError` [pydantic/concepts/json_schema.md].

The docs show this with a `CompressedString` dataclass:

```python
@classmethod
def __get_pydantic_core_schema__(
    cls, source: type[Any], handler: GetCoreSchemaHandler
) -> core_schema.CoreSchema:
    assert source is CompressedString
    return core_schema.no_info_after_validator_function(
        cls._validate,
        core_schema.str_schema(),
        serialization=core_schema.plain_serializer_function_ser_schema(
            cls._serialize,
            info_arg=False,
            return_schema=core_schema.str_schema(),
        ),
    )
```

Here `_validate` converts the input string into a `CompressedString`, and `_serialize` converts it back to a string [pydantic/concepts/json_schema.md]. The `str_schema()` input is what makes the field appear as a plain string. For this example, `MyModel.model_json_schema()` produces `{'properties': {'value': {'title': 'Value', 'type': 'string'}}, 'required': ['value'], 'title': 'MyModel', 'type': 'object'}` [pydantic/concepts/json_schema.md].

